          "examStrategy": "Unit 3 completes the syllabus's movement from the cosmos (Ṛta) through the State (Kautilya, the Mahabharata, Rāmarājya) to the world map of legal systems — and places India on it. David & Brierley's three families are the West's map; their own honesty admits it is incomplete and that \"the Muslim world, India, the Far East and Africa are far from having adhered to it without reservation.\" Sudame shows what sits outside that map: a legal system with its own constitution (Rājadharma), its own code of procedure (Vyavahāradharma), its own courts, judges and appellate hierarchy — and, most strikingly, its own sciences of research, evidence, reasoning and interpretation, which the Supreme Court of India has expressly held available to Indian courts today. Menon then grounds the whole argument in the working present: legal system as part of the social system, the Constitution self-given, the courts and the profession that administer it. The three readings are one argument: the Indian legal system is not an outpost of the Common law — it is a major legal system in its own right, and the Common law is only its received layer. Learn the map, learn the ancient system's architecture, quote the Mīmāṃsā and the two postulates — and write with the confidence of a student who knows both the borrowed and the native. Faculty of Law, University of Delhi, Jurisprudence – I (Legal Method), Course Code LB-106 , I Semester LL.B. (August 2025), Unit 3, pp. 78–107: (1) Rene David & J.E.C. Brierley, Major Legal Systems in the World Today (3rd edn., 1985), pp. 78–82; (2) Ila Sudame, \"The Shaastra of Law: Foundations from Indian Knowledge Systems for Contemporary Legal Methods,\" pp. 83–102; (3) N.R. Madhava Menon, \"Our Legal System,\" pp. 103–107. Cross-fed from the same casebook: Parasain, Dhyani, Seema Singh & Yadav, Dhavan (Unit 1, pp. 1–37) and the four Unit-2 readings (pp. 38–77). Authorities cited by the readings and cross-verified: A.S. Narayana Deekshitulu v. State of A.P. (1996) 9 SCC 548; Abhiraj Kuer v. Debendra Singh AIR 1962 SC 351; Dnyaneshwar B. Ganwat v. Karyakari Abhiyanta 2010 SCC OnLine Bom 766; Gujarat Urja Vikas Nigam Ltd. v. Essar Power Ltd. (2008) 4 SCC 755; B. Premchand v. Mohan Koikal (2011) 4 SCC 266; Surjit Singh v. MTNL (2009) 16 SCC 722; Kerala Solvent Extractions Ltd. v. A. Unnikrishnan (2006) 13 SCC 619; Sukhwinder Pal Bipan Kumar v. State of Punjab (1982) 1 SCC 31; Rajesh Kumar v. Rajendra Kumar Saxena 2012 SCC OnLine All 3966. Scholarship relied on by the readings: P.V. Kane; S.C. Banerji; M. Rama Jois; W.K. Lele; S.C. Chatterjee; R. Tripathi; K.L. Sarkar ( Mimansa Rules of Interpretation , Tagore Law Lectures); H.M. Seervai; Maxwell; Nāgeśa Bhaṭṭa ( Param Laghu Manjūṣā ). Texts: Jaimini Mīmāṃsā Sūtras (incl. 3:1:2, 6:3:9); Nyāya Sūtras (1.1.3; 1.2.4); Tarka Saṅgraha (50); Nārada Smriti; Brihadāraṇyaka Upanishad; Manusmriti; Parāshara Smriti; Arthashastra; and the Constitution of India, Penal Code, CrPC, CPC, Evidence Act 1872, Advocates Act 1961, Court Fees Act, Suits Valuation Act, Limitation Act, and the Law Commission of India's 77th Report.",
