          "examStrategy": "Unit-5 is a tale of four sources and one invisibility . Ancient India put dharma at the root and denied the king a legislative power — law was found, in texts, in usage, in conscience. Custom then became the great working source: the smṛtis' own commentators conceded it (\"custom decides everything\"), the British made it the rule of decision, and the cases — Moottoo Ramalinga to Munnalal — turned it into a case-law of its own. That case-law made precedent inevitable; and precedent, whose doctrine Dias unfolds with the ratio as a \"pointer within a broad spectrum of variations,\" supplied the machinery. Then came legislation — the one source the ancients withheld — and it subordinated all three: \"the future legal growth in India will be mostly due to legislation, and to some extent judicial interpretation and precedent.\" But the wheel does not simply turn: the Constitution is the modern dharma, binding every organ; the courts read statutes with Mīmāṃsā; and custom still lives in the savings, the tribal belts and the village. A Unit-5 answer that shows all four sources moving — and the ancient map inside the modern machinery — is a Unit-5 answer.",
