          "examStrategy": "Unit 6 is the story of one question asked three times: what makes an act of will into law? Austin answered with a sovereign: the command, the habit of obedience, the sanction — clean, rigorous, and, in the end, unable to bind its own author, unable to house powers, unable to explain continuity, unable to see that law is not merely obeyed but used . Hart repaired the theory by moving from orders to rules: primary rules carry the burdens, secondary rules carry the machinery (recognition, change, adjudication), and the internal point of view carries the meaning of obligation — but the repair costs the theory its own keystone, since the ultimate rule can neither be valid nor invalid, and \"its existence is a matter of fact.\" Kelsen then changed the grammar: not commands but norms, whose validity is an \"ought,\" whose foundation is a presupposition — the basic norm behind the historically first constitution — and whose test is effectiveness, not justice; the robber gang fails the test, the pirate state passes it, and the unjust state is law all the same. The three towers are three answers, three objections, and one lesson — that positivism's strength (method, purity, separation) and its vulnerability (the foundation that is willed, practiced or presupposed rather than justified) are the same fact. When Unit 7 arrives with natural law, Savigny and Pound, it will be attacking exactly this: the place where law stops asking for a reason. Keep that in mind, and no Unit-7 question will surprise you.",
