          "examStrategy": "Unit 7 is the casebook's answer to Unit 6, and the answer is threefold. If positivism said the law is what is posited , Fuller answers that positing has internal requirements — a system that cannot guide conduct makes no law, so the morality of law is not an add-on but the condition of law's possibility; and the Hart–Fuller debate, from Nuremberg to the one-child policy, is the cleanest demonstration that the \"is\" and the \"ought\" of law are, at the very least, mutually dependent. If positivism said the law is what is willed , Savigny answers that it is what is grown — the people's spirit, its customs, its history; and though Dias dismantles the Volksgeist in eleven steps, the residue survives as the permanent warning that reform without history is fragile. And if positivism said the law is what is valid , Pound answers that it is what works — an engineering of interests; and Dias's own critique supplies the missing half of the school, that what finally tells is not the list of interests but the ideal by which they are measured. Read whole, the unit is a single movement — \"moral authority\" (natural law) → \"social pressure\" (historical) → \"interests\" (sociological) — and the casebook hands you the sentence that joins them. Keep that sentence, the signature quotations, and the attacks that come with each doctrine, and every Unit-7 question — including the ones Unit 8 may throw at you — will find you ready.",
