          "anchorId": "dossier-3",
          "facts": "The Gupta and Singhania industrial groups had once jointly controlled the cotton-mill company, the aluminium corporation and a related firm. Their affairs had been intertwined. Following separation under an award, the corporation went to the Singhanias and the company/firm to the Guptas.\n\nThe cotton-mill company sued the corporation on accounts and obtained a decree for about ₹2,82,734, with costs and interest. The limitation issue turned on whether a corporation secretary’s letter validly acknowledged liability. The trial court accepted implied authority; the High Court reversed, holding the acknowledgement ineffective for extending limitation. The original decree had meanwhile been satisfied through a deposit and set-off arrangement.\n\nAfter reversal, the corporation obtained a restitution order dated 15 April 1967 for ₹4,11,554. The company appealed the underlying judgment to the Supreme Court, but had no stay of restitution and did not separately appeal the restitution order. Rather than establishing unsuccessful execution of process, the corporation served a three-week demand on 11 May 1967 and filed for winding up on 9 August 1967.\n\nThe mill had closed on 6 September 1966. Evidence concerned depression in the cotton industry, labour trouble, substantial liabilities, assets and possible government financial support. The petitioning corporation had sought to restrain the company from taking a proposed ₹40 lakh government loan intended to help restart the mill. The company asserted that the mill had resumed working profitably. Other creditor claims were also before the Court.",
