          "facts": "Two tobacco/cigarette manufacturers combined their businesses in a private company. They were its only two shareholders and only two directors, with equal voting power. Their relationship deteriorated so seriously that they would not speak to each other and communicated through the secretary. Disagreement affected important management and staffing matters. Yet the company remained solvent and profitable.\n\nThe ASIM summary notes an articles-based arbitration arrangement. The quoted appellate reasoning available externally treats arbitration as a means of resolving particular disputes, not as a workable substitute for the entire ongoing relationship of management and reasonable cooperation. A compulsory winding-up petition followed the complete breakdown. Source boundary: the uploaded summaries do not safely establish a complete chronology of every arbitral step or an authoritative allocation of every procedural role between the two principals; those details are not invented here. 3",
          "issues": "Whether ground for just and equitable winding up or substratum failure is made out.",
          "arguments": "Submissions on corporate solvency and commercial viability.",
          "ratio": "The company was ordered to be wound up on the just-and-equitable ground. The court treated the essential relationship as a partnership in the guise of a private company: the same fundamental inability to cooperate that could justify dissolving that personal association could justify corporate winding up. Prosperity did not cure the institutional and relational failure. 5\n\nThe appellate reasoning emphasised that the parties expected reasonable conduct and courtesy in working together. A mechanism for arbitrating specific disputes did not mean they intended the company to operate indefinitely through hostility and repeated arbitration. The court also resisted treating the examples of substratum failure and complete deadlock as exhaustive limits of equity. 3",
