          "ratio": "Outcome: the High Court rejected the constitutional challenge, declined interference with authorisation and dismissed the writ with ₹5 lakh costs, payable within four weeks according to the uploaded ASIM summary. It treated the proceedings as an abuse of process in the circumstances. A, printed pp. 108–109, corroborated by the reported extract. 2\n\n1. Different statutory categories were not interchangeable. The Registrar, who receives corporate information in an official capacity, occupies a different position from a person authorised to present a petition. The provision prescribing prior sanction and representation for the Registrar did not automatically extend to s. 272(1)(e). The reported conclusions at paragraphs 51–52 reject unconstitutionality and the requested reading down. 2\n\n2. Permission to approach the Tribunal is not itself winding up. The company could contest the case in adjudicatory proceedings. The authorisation did not amount to the final civil consequence of dissolution or a conclusive determination of all fraud allegations. The High Court’s reasoning stressed the opportunity to present objections before the competent forum. 2\n\n3. Mala fides were not established merely by chronology. The detailed request and material could not be brushed aside solely because authorisation came quickly, or because Antrix faced an award. The petitioner failed to establish the constitutional infringement alleged. 2\n\n4. Litigation conduct mattered. The shareholder had pursued the Tribunal route and sought participation, then initiated the writ immediately before the date fixed for final hearing. The Court characterised the parallel attack as abuse of process/proxy litigation in the particular circumstances. This is not a universal bar on constitutional challenges to company legislation. 2",
