          "file": "sem 3/company/DU_LLB_Company_Law_Topic_3_Constitutional_Documents.html",
          "anchorId": "pyq-u3-9",
          "question": "Model answer",
          "modelAnswer": "The doctrine of indoor management, also known as the rule in *Royal British Bank v. Turquand*, protects a bona fide person dealing with a company against a defect in the company’s *internal* procedure. Its starting point is the apparently harsh doctrine of constructive notice: an outsider is deemed to know the registered Memorandum and Articles and therefore cannot say that an express public restriction on corporate capacity or mode of execution was unknown. But the outsider is not required to find out whether a Board meeting was properly convened, whether quorum existed, whether a resolution was actually passed, or whether a permissible appointment or delegation was regularly made. In short: **the outsider must read the public rule, but need not audit its indoor observance.**"
