          "anchorId": "rev",
          "file": "sem 3/company/Oppression_Mismanagement_Topic7_Notes.html",
          "type": "Master Revision Capsule",
          "table": {
            "headers": [
              "Exception",
              "Ratio & leading authorities"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "① Acts ultra vires",
                "Object-clause boundaries are constitutional limits; “no majority of shareholders can sanction” what the company has no power to do — each member may sue for declaration/injunction (Bharat Insurance — Case 27; English precedent: Parke v. Daily News line; recovery suits vs officers who spent ultra vires money)."
              ],
              [
                "② Fraud on the minority",
                "“Fraud” = discriminatory use of majority power (Greenhalgh v. Arderne Cinemas: special resolution impeachable if it discriminates, giving the majority an advantage denied to the minority). Illustrations: Menier v. Hooper's Telegraph Works (majority settling company's claim to benefit a rival company they also owned — “put something into their pockets at the expense of the minority”); Cook v. Deeks (directors with ¾ votes diverting the company's contract to themselves — a “present to themselves” impossible); Brown v. British Abrasive Wheel (98% majority forcing buy-out of 2% — not “for the benefit of the company as a whole”, Allen v. Gold Reefs qualifier)."
              ],
              [
                "③ Acts requiring special majority",
                "What must be done by special resolution cannot be smuggled through by simple majority; members may restrain (Dhakeswari Cotton Mills v. Nil Kamal Chakravorty; Nagappa Chettiar v. Madras Race Club)."
              ],
              [
                "④ Wrongdoers in control",
                "Wigram VC foresaw it in Foss itself: “claims of justice would be found superior to any difficulties arising out of technical rules”. Where the persons complained against control the company (majority, or de facto dominance making a general meeting futile), a member may sue derivatively — the cause belongs to the company, the relief returns to it (Glass v. Atkin: equal split, defendants could block company action). “Control exists if it would be futile to call a general meeting because the wrongdoers would directly or indirectly exercise a decisive influence over the result.”"
              ],
              [
                "⑤ Individual membership rights",
                "Personal (not corporate) rights of membership: vote recorded (Pender v. Lushington, Jessel MR: “whether I vote with the majority or minority, I am entitled to have my vote recorded”); standing for election (Joseph v. Jos); proposing amendments; strict observance of legal/statutory/articles rules not waivable by bare majority."
              ],
              [
                "⑥ The statutory exception",
                "Ss. 241–246 themselves “are an exception to the rule in Foss v. Harbottle which lays down the sanctity of the majority rule” (Sinha J in Kanika Mukherji v. Rameshwar Dayal Dubey, Cal) — §3 below is this exception made flesh."
              ]
            ]
          },
