          "facts": "• Partnership: Manohar Lal (appellant) + Hiralal (respondent), Indore firm Diamond Industries (coal/cement, head office Indore); dissolved 22.8.45; Manohar to render true accounts, entitled to ¼ of Rs.4 lakh only after audit. Second proviso: “all disputes… decided amicably or in court at Indore and at nowhere else.”\n\n• Notices (1945): Hiralal alleges falsified accounts; Manohar denies, invites meeting at Asansol/Kajora.\n\n• Asansol Suit 18.8.48 (M.S.39/48): Manohar v. Hiralal for Rs.1,18,000; attaches dissolution deed. Hiralal: S.34 Arbitration stay (rejected 20.8.49); written statement 28.11.49 pleads no jurisdiction (Indore clause) + vexatious; issues 4.2.50 (jurisdiction + accounts). Hiralal’s Ss.10/151 stay (Dec.51) rejected 9.8.52 (“embarrassment ≠ abuse; S.10 inapplicable — Asansol earlier”); Calcutta HC affirms 7.5.53 + directs expeditious jurisdiction hearing.\n\n• Indore Suit 3.1.49 (COS 71/49): Hiralal v. Manohar for Rs.1,90,519 + render accounts. Manohar’s Ss.10/151 stay (28.4.50) rejected 5.7.51 (Indore clause; Asansol not “internal court” under Madhya Bharat CPC; different subject-matter); MB HC affirms 20.8.53.\n\n• Injunction (14.9.53): After both stays failed, Hiralal applies under S.151 in Indore to restrain Manohar from Asansol proceedings (alleges trouble/expense/time; violation of Indore clause). Suppresses Calcutta direction. ADJ Indore grants under O.39. MB HC (10.5.55) agrees O.39 R.1 inapplicable but sustains under S.151. Manohar appeals (SLP).",
          "issues": "• Whether court can exercise inherent powers when specific provisions (S.94 + O.39) exist for injunctions?\n• Whether inherent discretion was properly exercised on these facts?\n• (Arising at hearing) Legal effect of Indore forum clause on Asansol maintainability?",
          "arguments": "Appellant Manohar (injunction-opponent)Respondent Hiralal (injunction-holder) + MB HC view\n• S.94(c) + O.39 exhaustive; S.94 + O.39 cover injunctions → S.151 barred; allowing S.151 = override Legislature.\n• Even if power exists, misused: restraining earlier competent suit; defendant restraining plaintiff (inverse); no vexation proved; suppresses Calcutta order; forum clause ≠ ouster of jurisdiction.\n• Maqbul Ahmad (PC): no general discretion outside Act.• S.94 supplemental; O.39 permissive (“may”), not prohibitory; S.151 saves power for uncovered cases (Padam Sen).\n• Indore clause makes Asansol vexatious/oppressive; Asansol continuance violates agreement; Indore court as agreed forum may protect its proceedings via S.151.\n• MB HC: O.39 inapplicable but S.151 available.",
          "ratio": "• Q1 — Principle for Hiralal (power exists): Civil courts HAVE inherent jurisdiction to issue temporary injunctions in circumstances not covered by O.39. S.94 supplemental; O.39 Rr.1–2 do not say “not to be issued in other cases”; S.151 “nothing shall limit” — not controlled by Code (Padam Sen quoted); restriction only presumption that specific procedure dictated by justice; Maqbul Ahmad distinguished (Limitation Act has no saving clause like S.151). First contention repelled (para 24).\n\n• Q2 — Relief for Manohar (exercise improper): On facts, courts below erred (para 25). Inherent powers in very exceptional, no-procedure cases (para 26); restraining another regularly-constituted court needs great care, absolutely essential (para 27). Cohen: jurisdiction rarely exercised, great caution, applicant must show no advantage to defendant in other action; here defendant of earlier suit restraining plaintiff — inverse, no principle. Hyman: burden to show multiplicity vexatious; mere witnesses/contract/inconvenience ≠ vexation. Injunction set aside.\n\n• Forum clause: parties cannot confer/oust jurisdiction by agreement where court inherently lacks/has it; at best relevant to stay discretion, not to injunction as of right (para 15 third point; Takwani notes).",
          "examTips": "High-scoring precedent to cite in DU examination answers for Reference, Review, Revision & Inherent Powers."
