          "facts": "Detailed facts and procedural background of Punjab National Bank v. Surendra Prasad Sinha as prescribed in the DU case material.",
          "issues": "Key legal questions examined and determined by the Court in Punjab National Bank v. Surendra Prasad Sinha.",
          "arguments": "• s. 3 bars only the remedy, not the right: the debt subsisted despite limitation; the Bank was not obliged to sue at all.\n\n • The security bond expressly authorised appropriation from the FDR at maturity \"at your discretion\" and \"without reference to us\" — the Bank acted precisely as the contract permitted.\n\n • The FDR was entrusted as guarantor's security, not in the course of banking business — s. 409 IPC has no application; and action under contract negatives \"dishonesty\" (s. 405).\n\n • The complaint was a patent abuse of process to harass the Bank and its officers — the respondent was himself the Bank's Standing Counsel pursuing a personal vendetta.",
