          "unit": "Summary Procedure on Commercial Debts",
          "file": "sem 3/cpc/Summary_Procedure_Order_XXXVII_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "pyq-u6-1",
          "question": "What are the principles laid down by the Supreme Court in M/s. Mechalec Engineers & Manufacturers v. M/s. Basic Equipment Corporation, AIR 1977 SC 577, while dealing with the application for leave to defend in a suit filed by the plaintiff under Order XXXVII of the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908?",
          "modelAnswer": "Model Answer\n\n### 1. Introduction — the summary procedure\n\n Order XXXVII provides for **summary procedure**. A summary suit is one in which the defendant is **not entitled, as a matter of right, to defend the suit**, as in ordinary suits. He must apply for the court's **leave to defend** within the prescribed time (ten days from service of the summons for judgment — R.3(5); Art. 118, Limitation Act), and such leave is not granted if the defendant does not disclose facts indicating a **substantial defence**, or if his defence is **frivolous or vexatious**. Leave may be granted unconditionally or on such terms as the court deems just; if no leave is granted, the plaintiff is entitled to a decree.\n\n### 2. Scope and object\n\n The object of summary procedure is to **prevent unreasonable obstruction by a defendant who has no valid defence** and to secure the **expeditious disposal** of suits. Speedy decision is desirable \"in the interests of trade and commerce\" (*Santosh Kumar*, Bose J.).\n\n### 3. Procedure under Rules 2 and 3\n\n • **Rule 2:** plaint with the special averments and inscription; Form 4 summons; if the defendant does not enter appearance, the plaint's allegations are **deemed admitted** and the plaintiff obtains a decree **executable forthwith**.\n\n • **Rule 3:** service of plaint + appearance within ten days; **summons for judgment** (Form 4A) with the plaintiff's affidavit (cause of action, amount, and belief that there is no defence); application for leave within ten days disclosing sufficient facts; hearing and outcomes under R.3(6); delay excusable under R.3(7).\n\n### 4. When leave should be granted — the triable-issue principle\n\n Leave should be granted where there is a triable issue, i.e. — (a) a fair dispute to be tried as to the **meaning of the document** on which the claim is based; (b) **uncertainty as to the amount actually due**; (c) facts entitling the defendant to **interrogate** the plaintiff; or (d) to **cross-examine** his witnesses. Summary judgment should not be granted where there is a serious conflict as to matters of fact or difficulty on issues of law; the defence should not be rejected merely for inherent implausibility or inconsistency (**Raj Duggal v. Ramesh Kumar Bansal**, AIR 1990 SC 2218).\n\n### 5. Facts, issue and decision of Mechalec\n\n • **Facts:** a registered partnership firm sued on a dishonoured cheque for ₹21,265.28 + ₹7,655 interest, alleging it was given as the price of goods supplied; the defendant admitted issuing the cheque (by its managing partner) but denied privity of contract. Under O.37, the trial court (Additional District Judge, Delhi) granted **unconditional leave**; the Delhi High Court in revision (s.115) held the defences not bona fide and granted leave only on deposit of the amount with interest and costs.\n\n • **Issue:** could the High Court, under s.115, interfere with the trial court's discretion granting unconditional leave on grounds which the HC's own order showed to be reasonable?\n\n • **Decision:** the SC held that deciding bona fides as a pure question of fact **before evidence** is hazardous; only where the defence is **patently dishonest or so unreasonable that it could not reasonably be expected to succeed** may unconditional leave be questioned. The HC had ignored settled principles of s.115 jurisdiction; its order was set aside and the trial court's restored.\n\n### 6. The principles stated in Mechalec (adopting Das J. in Kiranmoyee Dassi)\n\n • **Good defence on merits** → plaintiff not entitled to sign judgment; defendant entitled to **unconditional leave**.\n\n • **Trible issue** — fair/bona fide/reasonable defence though not positively good → **unconditional leave**.\n\n • **Facts sufficient to entitle him to defend** (defence may possibly be established at trial) → leave, with conditions possible only **as to time or mode of trial**, not payment into court or security.\n\n • **No defence / illusory / sham / \"practically moonshine\"** → ordinarily, plaintiff entitled to sign judgment; no leave.\n\n • **Even where there is no defence**, the court may protect the plaintiff by allowing the defence to proceed only if the **amount claimed is paid into court or secured** — mercy to the defendant.\n\n In *Mechalec* the case did not fall within class (e); **only in that class is a deposit condition justifiable**.\n\n### 7. Conclusion (with the modern position)\n\n *Mechalec* remains the leading exposition of the leave-to-defend framework and of revisional restraint. However, after the **1976 amendment** (w.e.f. 1-2-1977) substituted Rule 3, the Supreme Court in **IDBI Trusteeship Services Ltd. v. Hubtown Ltd.** (2016) held that the propositions in paragraph 8 of *Mechalec* stand **superseded**: today, even where triable issues are raised, conditions (including deposit/security) may be imposed if the judge doubts the defendant's good faith; a \"plausible but improbable\" defence may attract conditions up to the **entire principal with interest**; and the **admitted amount must in any case be deposited** (second proviso; *Southern Sales*). A complete answer states *Mechalec* and then notes *IDBI Trusteeship*."
