        {
          "id": "wcc-case-u6-1",
          "name": "Harjit Singh v. State of Punjab",
          "citation": "(2011) 4 SCC 441 · Supreme Court of India · Author: Dr. B.S. Chauhan, J. (Division Bench with P. Sathasivam, J.) · Decided 30 March 2011",
          "bench": "Supreme Court of India",
          "unitNumber": 6,
          "unit": "The Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985",
          "file": "sem 3/wcc/NDPS_Act_1985_DU_Comprehensive_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "case1",
          "facts": "• On 4-7-2003, a police party was going from Focal Point, Mandi Gobindgarh towards the G.T. Road on patrol in a government vehicle. Near a culvert in village Ambe Majra, they spotted the appellant coming on foot with a plastic bag in his right hand; on seeing the police he turned to the left. He was apprehended on suspicion.\n\n • One Ashok Kumar, an independent witness, arrived and joined the party. The appellant was apprised of his s.50 right to be searched before a Gazetted Officer and his statement was recorded. The ASP, Shri Dinesh Partap Singh, was summoned; in his presence Inspector Amarjit Singh (PW-3) searched the bag — it contained opium.\n\n • Two samples of 10 g each were drawn; the remaining opium weighed 7.10 kg. Samples and stock were sealed and taken into possession. ₹510 was recovered on personal search; arrest memo prepared; FIR registered.\n\n • The FSL report confirmed the substance was opium with 0.8% morphine. Charge-sheet under s.18 followed; the appellant claimed trial.\n\n • At trial the prosecution examined PW-1 Constable Manjinder Singh, PW-2 HC Jagdish Singh, PW-3 Inspector Amarjit Singh, PW-4 ASP Dinesh Partap Singh and PW-5 SI Dalip Singh. The independent witness Ashok Kumar was not examined as he had been “won over”. In his s.313 CrPC statement the appellant alleged false implication, claimed he was lifted from home, ₹6,000 snatched, and that he could barely walk after a 1999 accident; he examined six defence witnesses.",
          "issues": "Whether, where recovered opium weighs 7.10 kg but contains only 0.8% morphine (56.96 g), the sentence must be graded by the morphine content (which sat above the small-quantity threshold for morphine but below its commercial threshold), making the maximum 10-year sentence unwarranted — or by the weight of the opium itself.",
          "arguments": "• Reliance on E. Micheal Raj v. Intelligence Officer, NCB, (2008) 5 SCC 161: when a drug is found mixed with neutral substance(s), only the content of the narcotic drug is taken into account for punishment.\n\n • 0.8% of 7.10 kg = 56.96 g morphine — above the 0.5 g minimum but below the 250 g commercial threshold for morphine: an intermediate band, hence the maximum sentence was illegal.",
          "ratio": "Principle / ratio\n ① Classify before sentencing: identify the substance and apply its own Notification entry. ② For pure opium (coagulated juice, s.2(xv)(a)), the entire weight counts; morphine percentage is irrelevant to the small/commercial band (Entry 92). ③ Morphine assay matters only for mixtures under s.2(xv)(b) or to bring a preparation within the opium-derivative definition s.2(xvi) (e.g. &gt;0.2% morphine). ④ A notification whose effect enhances punishment cannot apply retrospectively (Art. 20(1)).",
          "examTips": "Important precedent to cite for scoring high marks in DU examinations."
        },
        {
          "id": "wcc-case-u6-2",
          "name": "Hira Singh and Another v. Union of India",
          "citation": "(2020) 20 SCC 272 · Three-Judge Bench, Supreme Court · Author: M.R. Shah, J. · Reference answered 2020",
          "bench": "Supreme Court of India",
          "unitNumber": 6,
          "unit": "The Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985",
          "file": "sem 3/wcc/NDPS_Act_1985_DU_Comprehensive_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "case2",
          "facts": "Essential factual background of Hira Singh and Another v. Union of India as analyzed in DU White Collar Crimes notes.",
          "issues": "Substantive question of law and statutory interpretation framed in Hira Singh and Another v. Union of India.",
          "arguments": "• E. Micheal Raj read only s.21 in isolation; the expression “offending material” appears nowhere in the Act; reliance on Ouseph v. State of Kerala, (2004) 4 SCC 446 was misplaced as that decision passed sub silentio.\n\n • Legislative history: the original s.2(xx) defined “preparation” (one or more drugs in dosage/solution/mixture); “mixture” = mechanical mixture of two or more substances; s.2(xxiii) psychotropic substance included preparations (Schedule Entry 77 residuary, now 111); narcotic drugs s.2(xiv) included coca leaf, cannabis, opium, poppy straw and manufactured drugs — the Act always covered preparations, not merely pure content.\n\n • Originally only s.27 recognised a “small quantity” (S.O. 827 of 14-11-1985, five drugs); the 1989 amendment did not alter the scheme; the 2001 amendment added s.2(vii-a) and (xxiii-a) to rationalise sentencing — emphasis remained on quantity of the drug/substance including preparations; s.37 was similarly amended and ss.27/64A reworked (possession left s.27; addict immunity narrowed).\n\n • Note 2 of the 2001 Notification expressly applied notified quantities to preparations; Note 4 (2009) merely stated the obvious (ex abundanti cautela) — even ignoring it, the Act reached the entire mixture/solution.\n\n • Chapman v. United States, US Supreme Court (LSD blotter paper; 50 mg LSD on 5.7 g paper): the sentence turned on the weight of the “mixture or substance containing a detectable amount” — the street weight, not pure content; “that is a rational sentencing scheme”.\n\n • Practical absurdity: small quantity of heroin is 5 g; pure-content theory would make 100 g of street heroin (~0.25 g/dose ≈ 400 doses) a small-quantity offence with six months — traffickers would escape; the object and Preamble demand a literal deterrent reading (Crawford on Statutory Construction; Maxwell via Directorate of Enforcement v. Deepak Mahajan, (1994) 3 SCC 440; Murlidhar Meghraj Loya; Reema Aggarwal; Rajinder Singh — fair, pragmatic, common-sense construction; ex visceribus actus). Thus s.21 is not standalone; E. Micheal Raj is per incuriam.",
          "ratio": "Principle / ratio\n Sentencing quantity is the “street weight” — the total weight of the seized mixture/preparation/solution/dosage form, including neutral/cutting material — not the assay-pure narcotic content. This applies to manufactured drugs (s.21) and psychotropic substances (s.22); for a mixture of two or more notified drugs the lowest constituent threshold (Entry 239) governs. Note 4 is declarative of the pre-existing law. Government notifications cannot reduce thresholds inconsistently, but counting the full seized preparation is within ss.2(vii-a)/(xxiii-a) because the Act itself punishes preparations (s.2(xx)).",
          "examTips": "Important precedent to cite for scoring high marks in DU examinations."
        },
        {
          "id": "wcc-case-u6-3",
          "name": "Karnail Singh v. State of Haryana",
          "citation": "(2009) 8 SCC 539 · Constitution Bench (5 Judges), Supreme Court · Author: P. Sathasivam, J. · Decided 29 July 2009",
          "bench": "Supreme Court of India",
          "unitNumber": 6,
          "unit": "The Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985",
          "file": "sem 3/wcc/NDPS_Act_1985_DU_Comprehensive_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "case3",
          "facts": "Essential factual background of Karnail Singh v. State of Haryana as analyzed in DU White Collar Crimes notes.",
          "issues": "Substantive question of law and statutory interpretation framed in Karnail Singh v. State of Haryana.",
          "arguments": "Submissions of the respective parties before the Court in Karnail Singh v. State of Haryana.",
          "ratio": "Principle / ratioSection 42 is mandatory in its core (reduce information to writing; copy to superior) but admits temporally relaxed compliance in genuine on-the-spot emergencies where acting later would allow escape/destruction; total omission or in-station inaction is fatal to the prosecution’s credibility (recovery becomes suspect, prejudice presumed in practice), while explained delay is not.",
          "examTips": "Important precedent to cite for scoring high marks in DU examinations."
        },
        {
          "id": "wcc-case-u6-4",
          "name": "Vijaysinh Chandubha Jadeja (and Another) v. State of Gujarat (and Others)",
          "citation": "(2011) 1 SCC 609 · Constitution Bench, Supreme Court · Author: D.K. Jain, J. · Decided 29 October 2010",
          "bench": "Supreme Court of India",
          "unitNumber": 6,
          "unit": "The Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985",
          "file": "sem 3/wcc/NDPS_Act_1985_DU_Comprehensive_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "case4",
          "facts": "Essential factual background of Vijaysinh Chandubha Jadeja (and Another) v. State of Gujarat (and Others) as analyzed in DU White Collar Crimes notes.",
          "issues": "Substantive question of law and statutory interpretation framed in Vijaysinh Chandubha Jadeja (and Another) v. State of Gujarat (and Others).",
          "arguments": "For the accused (Shri P.H. Parekh, Sr.)\n\n Conjoint reading of s.50(1) & (3) shows the safeguard must be employed effectively and honestly; Baldev Singh unambiguously settled the duty to inform; Joseph Fernandez and Prabha Shankar Dubey wrongly imported “substantial compliance”.\n Section 50 is the only safeguard against the statute’s extensive powers — a “due process” clause with minimum procedural limits; per Beckodan Abdul Rahiman v. State of Kerala, (2002) 4 SCC 229, the harshness of NDPS casts a heavier duty on the prosecution. Mere questioning can never amount to compliance; it would make the right illusory.\n\nFor the States / Union (Shri Siddharth Luthra; Shri P.P. Malhotra, ASG)\n\n Section 50’s rigours do not apply to officers acting on a s.41(1) Magistrate’s warrant or to Gazetted Officers acting/authorising under s.41(2) — Parliament reposes higher trust in gazetted rank (reliance on M. Prabhulal (2003) 8 SCC 449; UOI v. Satrohan (2008) 8 SCC 313; State of Rajasthan v. Ram Chandra (2005) 5 SCC 151 — the right is to be taken before the GO/Magistrate, who then decides discharge/direction to search).\n Balbir Singh, Saiyad Umar Saiyad, Ali Mustaffa cases wrongly “read the phrase ‘for making the search’ into s.50(1)”, making it over-rigorous; Baldev Singh needed reconsideration; the Court should accept substantial compliance.\n\n The Court confined the reference to the scope of the words “if the person to be searched so requires” and did not adjudicate the s.41 applicability arguments (noting Baldev Singh had itself considered s.41).",
          "ratio": "Principle / ratio“Inform, don’t merely ask.” The suspect must be made to understand that the choice to be searched before a GO/Magistrate is his right, not the officer’s concession. Non-information does not per se void the trial, but personal recovery in such a search becomes suspect evidence and cannot sustain a conviction based solely on it; emergency s.50(5) searches need recorded reasons + 72-hour report and are exceptional. Applied later in Arif Khan @ Agha Khan v. State of Uttarakhand (2018) to acquit where mere written consent was obtained without full compliance.",
          "examTips": "Important precedent to cite for scoring high marks in DU examinations."
        },
        {
          "id": "wcc-case-u6-5",
          "name": "Union of India v. Mohanlal & Another",
          "citation": "(2016) 3 SCC 379 · Division Bench, Supreme Court · Author: T.S. Thakur, J. · Judgment 28 January 2016 (interlocutory directions from 2012, (2012) SCC orders)",
          "bench": "Supreme Court of India",
          "unitNumber": 6,
          "unit": "The Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985",
          "file": "sem 3/wcc/NDPS_Act_1985_DU_Comprehensive_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "case5",
          "facts": "• The Union appealed against an MP High Court acquittal of the respondents under ss.8/18(b) r/w s.29: the HC found the prosecution had produced no evidence that the 3.36 kg opium seized had been destroyed/disposed of under the prescribed procedure; failure to produce the contraband before the trial court implied failure to prove seizure/possession.\n\n • The Centre relied on Standing Order No. 1/89 (13-6-1989) governing seizure, sampling, safe-keeping and disposal, said to be followed nationwide, and a Department of Revenue Circular of 23-2-2011 exhorting Chief Secretaries/police chiefs to enforce it. Doubts persisted because pilferage and re-circulation of seized drugs is a major hazard.\n\n • The Court appointed Shri Ajit Kumar Sinha, Senior Advocate, as Amicus Curiae to review the working of the procedure and identify loopholes.\n\n • A Times of India report (“Bathinda’s police stores bursting at the seams…”) documented, in Bathinda alone, 39 lakh sedative/narcotic tablets, 1.10 lakh capsules, 21,000 drug syrups, 1,828 injections, 8 kg smack, 84 kg ganja awaiting disposal — indicative of nationwide accumulation, worst in border States.",
          "issues": "• Section 52A(1) empowers the Centre to notify the disposal procedure; Standing Order 1/89 para 2.2 required on-the-spot duplicate samples drawn before panchas and the person, noted in the panchnama. The survey showed no uniform practice — only the DRI claimed spot sampling; the NCB said it did not; most States did not.\n\n • The Court set out s.52A (as substituted in 2014): on seizure and forwarding to the SHO/s.53 officer, an inventory must be prepared and an application made to a Magistrate for (a) certification of inventory, (b) photographs certified true, (c) representative samples drawn in the Magistrate’s presence with certified sample lists; the Magistrate must allow “as soon as may be”; certified documents/samples are primary evidence under s.52A(4) notwithstanding the Evidence Act/CrPC.\n\n • Held: the statutory scheme does not contemplate sampling at seizure (which usually occurs without a Magistrate); sampling is to be under Magistrate supervision and certification. Where Standing Order 1/89 conflicts with the statute, the statute prevails; the Centre was asked to re-examine/recalibrate the Standing Order. Seizure must be followed by the s.52A(2) application without undue delay; Magistrates must act expeditiously (s.52A(3)); High Courts must supervise both Magistrates and agencies.",
          "arguments": "Submissions of the respective parties before the Court in Union of India v. Mohanlal & Another.",
          "ratio": "Principle / ratio① The NDPS sampling event is Magistrate-centric: s.52A(2)–(4) makes his supervised, certified inventory/photographs/representative samples the primary evidence; spot sampling is not statutorily mandated and a subordinate Standing Order cannot override the statute. ② Seized narcotics are hazardous, theft/ substitution-vulnerable evidence: the State owes a chain-of-custody duty — timely application, secure double-lock vaults, registers, inspections, DDC destruction under s.52A/2015 Notification. ③ Courts have constitutional supervisory jurisdiction (Arts. 226/227/141–142-style continuing mandamus) to compel systemic reform in NDPS administration, monitored by High Court Judges’ Committees.",
          "examTips": "Important precedent to cite for scoring high marks in DU examinations."
        },
        {
          "id": "wcc-case-u6-6",
          "name": "Indian Harm Reduction Network & Another v. Union of India & Others (with Gulam Mohammed Malik matters)",
          "citation": "Bombay High Court, Writ/PIL jurisdiction (Art. 226) · Division Bench: A.M. Khanwilkar & A.P. Bhangale, JJ. · Decided 16 June 2011",
          "bench": "Supreme Court of India",
          "unitNumber": 6,
          "unit": "The Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985",
          "file": "sem 3/wcc/NDPS_Act_1985_DU_Comprehensive_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "case6",
          "facts": "Essential factual background of Indian Harm Reduction Network & Another v. Union of India & Others (with Gulam Mohammed Malik matters) as analyzed in DU White Collar Crimes notes.",
          "issues": "• Does s.31A violate Article 14? — No. The classification (first-time offender vs repeat offender dealing in specified huge quantities; or offences under ss.19/24/27A) is founded on intelligible differentia with rational nexus to deterrence; the classification between s.31 and s.31A cases is also rational.\n\n • Does it violate Article 21? — Yes, as a mandatory sentence. Maneka Gandhi requires procedure that is just, fair and reasonable. Standardised mandatory death: (a) eliminates the pre-sentence hearing under s.235(2) CrPC; (b) does away with special reasons under s.354(3) CrPC and the Bachan Singh “rarest of rare” norm; (c) destroys individualised sentencing (circumstances of offence and offender); (d) impinges on separation of powers and the judicial sentencing function; (e) is disproportionate — drug activities are licensed torts absent licence, not inherently heinous, and drugs serve medical/scientific needs; (f) offends Article 6 ICCPR (no arbitrary deprivation of life).\n\n • Is s.31A void ab initio? — No. The provision was saved by reading it down prospectively.\n\n • Can “shall” be read as “may”? — Yes. “Shall be punishable with death” is to be read as “may be punishable with death”: the court retains discretion to impose the s.31 enhanced term or, on special reasons, death.",
          "arguments": "Submissions of the respective parties before the Court in Indian Harm Reduction Network & Another v. Union of India & Others (with Gulam Mohammed Malik matters).",
          "ratio": "Principle / ratio and aftermathNo mandatory death penalty for drug offences: sentencing discretion and the rarest-of-rare discipline are non-negotiable under Article 21. Parliament gave effect to the ruling via Act 16 of 2014 (w.e.f. 1-5-2014): s.31A now prescribes “punishment which shall not be less than the punishment specified in s.31 or with death” — death optional. Pre-2014, the same result followed by judicial reading-down. Parallels: Mithu Singh v. State of Punjab struck down s.303 IPC mandatory death (s.303 IPC has no counterpart in the BNS, 2023).",
          "examTips": "Important precedent to cite for scoring high marks in DU examinations."
        }
